  "atomType": "R",
  "theory": {
        "intro": "A calculator stores the value of every trigonometric ratio. Once the angle is known, a few keystrokes turn $\\sin\\theta$, $\\cos\\theta$ or $\\tan\\theta$ into a decimal, which is then rounded as the question asks.",
        "facts": [
"The calculator must be in **degree** mode when entering an angle as a bare number. Entering $20$ with the sine key in radian mode gives approximately $0.9129$; the explicitly degree-labelled angle gives $\\sin 20^{\\circ}\\approx 0.3420$.",
"For an acute angle, $\\sin\\theta$ and $\\cos\\theta$ lie between $0$ and $1$, so a value above $1$ signals a keying error.",
"$\\tan\\theta$ has no upper limit: $\\tan 64^{\\circ}\\approx 2.0503$ and $\\tan 82.3^{\\circ}\\approx 7.3962$.",
"In $12\\sin 13^{\\circ}$ the $12$ multiplies the ratio, so evaluate $\\sin 13^{\\circ}$ and then multiply.",
"In $\\dfrac{9}{\\sin 57^{\\circ}}$ the ratio is the divisor, so evaluate $\\sin 57^{\\circ}$ and then divide $9$ by it.",
"Round once, at the very end, because rounding the ratio first shifts the last digits of the answer."
],
        "steps": [
"Check the calculator is in degree mode",
"Evaluate the trigonometric ratio",
"Complete any multiplication or division, keeping full precision",
"Round to the number of decimal places asked for"
]
      },
